    "   \"cell_type\": \"markdown\",\n",
    "   \"metadata\": {},\n",
    "   \"source\": [\n",
    "    \"### Answer Key Questions:\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"#### 1. Experience Replay Buffer:\\n\",\n",
    "    \"- **Does early training help or hurt?**\\n\",\n",
    "    \"  - Early training (small min_buffer_size) can lead to unstable learning\\n\",\n",
    "    \"  - Larger min_buffer_size provides more diverse experiences before training\\n\",\n",
    "    \"  - Trade-off: faster start vs. stability\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"- **How does buffer size affect stability?**\\n\",\n",
    "    \"  - Larger buffers: More stable but slower to adapt\\n\",\n",
    "    \"  - Smaller buffers: Less stable but adapts faster\\n\",\n",
    "    \"  - Sweet spot around 50k-100k for LunarLander\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"#### 2. Exploration Strategy:\\n\",\n",
    "    \"- **What happens if exploration decays too quickly?**\\n\",\n",
    "    \"  - Agent converges to suboptimal policy\\n\",\n",
    "    \"  - Insufficient exploration of state space\\n\",\n",
    "    \"  - Higher variance in performance\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"- **Are DQN and Double DQN affected differently?**\\n\",\n",
    "    \"  - Double DQN more robust to fast decay\\n\",\n",
    "    \"  - Better value estimates allow learning from less exploration\\n\",\n",
    "    \"  - DQN needs more exploration to overcome overestimation\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"#### 3. Target Network Updates:\\n\",\n",
    "    \"- **Hard Updates:**\\n\",\n",
    "    \"  - Clear separation between online and target\\n\",\n",
    "    \"  - Can cause periodic instability\\n\",\n",
    "    \"  - Works well with frequency 1000-2000 steps\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"- **Soft Updates:**\\n\",\n",
    "    \"  - Smoother learning\\n\",\n",
    "    \"  - More stable but slower convergence\\n\",\n",
    "    \"  - τ around 0.005 works best\\n\",\n",
    "    \"\\n\",\n",
    "    \"#### 4. DQN vs Double DQN:\\n\",\n",
    "    \"- **Learning Speed:** Double DQN often converges faster\\n\",\n",
    "    \"- **Stability:** Double DQN more stable across hyperparameters\\n\",\n",
    "    \"- **Final Performance:** Similar, but Double DQN more consistent\\n\",\n",
    "    \"- **Q-Values:** DQN overestimates, Double DQN more accurate\\n\",\n",
    "    \"- **Robustness:** Double DQN less sensitive to hyperparameters\"\n",
    "   ]\n",
    "  },\n",
